@page "/docs/maui"
@layout Layout.DocsLayout

<PageTitle>MAUI and Blazor Hybrid · blux-ui</PageTitle>
<div class="pg-prose">
    <p class="pg-eyebrow">Getting started</p>
    <h1>MAUI and Blazor Hybrid</h1>
    <p class="pg-lead">Blux runs in a <code>BlazorWebView</code>, so one set of pages can ship as a web app and as an app for Android, Windows, iOS and macOS. The <code>maui-blazor-web</code> template (a shared Razor class library, a web project and a MAUI project) is the intended starting point; <code>samples/Blux.Maui</code> in the repository is that template with Blux in it.</p>

    <p>The two screens below are the sample app in the Android emulator: a phone in dark mode, and Android TV in light mode with a switch focused by the D-pad.</p>
    <div class="not-prose my-6 grid items-end gap-6 sm:grid-cols-[minmax(0,13rem)_minmax(0,1fr)]">
        <figure class="m-0 w-full max-w-52 sm:justify-self-start">
            <BxImage Src="img/platform-phone-home-dark.webp" Provider="none" Alt="The home page of the sample app on an Android phone, dark mode: a card reports the WebView renderer, the Phone form factor and Android 16." Width="540" Height="1200" Loading="BxImageLoading.Lazy" Class="block h-auto w-full rounded-[1.75rem] border-4 border-(--blux-border-accented)" data-testid="maui-phone" />
            <figcaption class="mt-2 text-xs text-muted">Android phone</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <figure class="m-0 w-full">
            <BxImage Src="img/platform-tv-forms-light.webp" Provider="none" Alt="The Forms page of the sample app on Android TV, light mode: a thick blue focus ring around the Email notifications switch." Width="1280" Height="720" Loading="BxImageLoading.Lazy" Class="block h-auto w-full rounded-lg border-4 border-(--blux-border-accented)" data-testid="maui-tv" />
            <figcaption class="mt-2 text-xs text-muted">Android TV, TV mode</figcaption>
        </figure>
    </div>

    <h2>Status by platform</h2>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Web</strong>: all four Blazor render modes (static SSR, Server, WebAssembly, Auto), with the same markup.</li>
        <li><strong>Android</strong> (phone): built and tested on the emulator.</li>
        <li><strong>iOS 17 and later</strong> (the native popover is required), <strong>Windows</strong> and <strong>macOS</strong> (Mac Catalyst): CI build jobs are set up but have not run yet, and nothing has been run on a device.</li>
        <li><strong>Android TV</strong>: experimental, tested on the Android TV emulator. The soft keyboard has not been seen yet: the TV emulator has no on-screen keyboard, so check it on a real TV.</li>
        <li><strong>Tizen</strong> (Samsung TV): not supported, there is no BlazorWebView there.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>What a web view changes</h2>
    <ul>
        <li>The renderer is <code>RendererInfo.Name == "WebView"</code>: always interactive, no prerendering, no <code>HttpContext</code>, no server to render the <code>&lt;head&gt;</code>. <code>BluxHost.IsHybrid(RendererInfo)</code> tells, and every component that depends on a server degrades by itself.</li>
        <li>Only the classic JS initializer exports run (<code>beforeStart</code> and <code>afterStarted</code>). Blux exports both, next to <code>afterWebStarted</code> for Blazor Web Apps, so custom elements, the <code>bxtoggle</code> and <code>bxchange</code> events and the color mode are set up in every host.</li>
        <li>Static web assets of the library are served at <code>_content/Blux.UI/...</code> from the app's <code>wwwroot/index.html</code>, like in a browser.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Set up</h2>
    <p>Reference the package from the shared library and the MAUI project, call <code>AddBluxUi</code> from code both hosts run, and use the same layout everywhere.</p>
    <CodeBlock Title="MauiProgram.cs" Code="@Program" />
    <p>The host page links the stylesheets. <code>viewport-fit=cover</code> lets the page use the whole screen; Blux keeps headers, panels and toasts inside the safe area (<code>env(safe-area-inset-*)</code>).</p>
    <CodeBlock Title="wwwroot/index.html" Code="@Index" />
    <p>
        <strong>Android 15 and later draw apps edge to edge</strong>, so the web view would sit under the status bar and the gesture bar, and taps on a header would land on the system bar.
        The Android web view does not reliably report <code>env(safe-area-inset-*)</code> (those insets stay 0), so on Android let MAUI inset the page: set
        <code>SafeAreaEdges="Container"</code> on the <code>ContentPage</code> that hosts the <code>BlazorWebView</code> (.NET MAUI 10). On iOS and Mac Catalyst the CSS insets do work with
        <code>viewport-fit=cover</code>, and Blux uses them there; do not combine both on one platform or the space is added twice.
    </p>
    <p>The page sends content to the head through <code>HeadOutlet</code>. Add it as a root component with the selector <code>head::after</code>, and place <code>BxHead</code> once in the layout:</p>
    <CodeBlock Title="MainPage.xaml" Code="@Xaml" />
    <p><code>BxHead</code> renders the theme variables of <code>AddBluxUi(o =&gt; o.Colors...)</code> (<code>BxThemeStyle</code>) and the self-hosted fonts (<code>BxFonts</code>) into the head. It renders nothing in a browser, where <code>App.razor</code> places them. The color mode needs nothing: the JS initializer applies the stored mode before the app renders, so <code>BxColorModeScript</code> renders nothing in a web view and <code>BxCultureMeta</code> neither.</p>

    <h2>System bars and the Back button (Android)</h2>
    <p>
        With edge to edge, the status bar and the gesture bar show whatever is behind the page: the MAUI page background. To make them follow the color mode, the sample watches
        <code>bx-color-mode</code> (Blux dispatches it on every change), passes <code>dark</code> to the host, and the MAUI side sets the page background to <code>--blux-bg</code>
        and <code>AppearanceLightStatusBars</code> / <code>AppearanceLightNavigationBars</code> on <code>WindowCompat.GetInsetsController</code> (see <code>SystemBars</code> in the sample).
    </p>
    <p>
        Since Android 13 Back is not a key event but goes through <code>OnBackPressedDispatcher</code>, and with no callback the system leaves the app (on a TV, to the launcher) even with a dialog open.
        Register a callback in <code>MainActivity.OnCreate</code> that evaluates <code>Blux.tvBack()</code> in the web view: it leaves the editing mode of a field, else closes the topmost dialog or popover (the one opened last; a modal that is not <code>dismissible</code> swallows Back, exactly like Escape), else goes back in the history, and
        returns <code>false</code> only when nothing is left, which is when the app goes to the background.
    </p>

    <h2>Fonts and icons</h2>
    <p>Declare <code>BluxFont</code> items and import the Blux targets in the <strong>MAUI project</strong> (a package reference does it for you). The fonts are static web assets under <code>_blux/fonts/</code>, which MAUI copies into the app as assets served from <code>wwwroot</code>; <code>BxFonts</code> finds its manifest in the loaded assemblies (a MAUI app has no entry assembly on every platform) and links the plain route, because a web view has no asset fingerprint map. Icons are bundled by name at build time as on the web; add the shared project with <code>&lt;BluxIconScanDirectory Include="..\Shared" /&gt;</code>.</p>

    <h2>Images</h2>
    <p>The default provider stays <code>none</code>: images keep their <code>src</code>, files under <code>wwwroot</code> load from the device. CDN providers (<code>Blux.UI.Images</code>) work as on the web. <code>Blux.UI.Images.Local</code> is an ASP.NET Core endpoint and is web only. <code>BxImage Preload</code> adds no head link in a web view (the image still loads eagerly).</p>

    <h2>Language</h2>
    <p>The culture of a .NET process comes from the device, and changing it takes effect after a restart. <code>BxLocaleSelect</code> in a web view writes no cookie and does not reload: it saves the choice in an <code>IBxCultureStore</code> (the <code>localStorage</code> of the web view by default) and <code>BxApp</code> applies it before its content renders. Set <code>Reload="true"</code> to apply it at once (the process culture changes and the web view reloads). To start in the right language with no flash, keep the choice in MAUI <code>Preferences</code> and read it in <code>MauiProgram</code>:</p>
    <CodeBlock Title="PreferencesCultureStore.cs" Code="@Store" />
    <p><code>BxCultureMeta</code> and <code>ApplyServerCultureAsync</code> are server features: they do nothing in a web view.</p>

    <h2>Storage</h2>
    <p>Color mode, the banner, the dashboard sizes and, in a web view, the sidebar state use <code>localStorage</code>, which works on every platform. Cookies are unreliable in a web view (WebKit's tracking prevention on iOS and macOS) and are not used there. The sidebar and dashboard cannot be rendered with their remembered state on a server, because there is none.</p>

    <h2>Forms</h2>
    <p>A <code>FormName</code> is only how a form posts in a Blazor Web App without interactivity. A web view is always interactive, so <code>BxForm</code>, <code>BxAuthForm</code> and <code>BxChatPrompt</code> submit through their events; synchronous JS interop is not available, and Blux does not use it.</p>

    <h2>Touch</h2>
    <ul>
        <li>Tooltips ignore the tap; they open on a long press or on keyboard focus.</li>
        <li>On a coarse pointer, buttons, tabs and other controls get a hit area of at least 44 by 44 pixels; rows of a list (menu items, options) keep their own height. Add <code>data-bx-compact</code> to opt an element out.</li>
        <li>Nothing is reachable only by hover.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Platforms</h2>
    <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-default">
        <table class="w-full min-w-xl text-start text-sm">
            <caption class="sr-only">Platform support</caption>
            <thead class="bg-muted text-highlighted">
                <tr><th scope="col" class="px-3 py-2 text-start">Platform</th><th scope="col" class="px-3 py-2 text-start">Minimum</th><th scope="col" class="px-3 py-2 text-start">Status</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><th scope="row" class="px-3 py-2 text-start">Android</th><td class="px-3 py-2">7.0 (API 24), with an up to date Android System WebView (Chromium 114 or later for the popover API)</td><td class="px-3 py-2">Built and run on an emulator</td></tr>
                <tr><th scope="row" class="px-3 py-2 text-start">iOS</th><td class="px-3 py-2"><strong>17</strong> (MAUI itself: 16.4). Blux uses the native popover API, which WebKit ships from 17</td><td class="px-3 py-2">CI build job set up, not run yet</td></tr>
