            </tbody>
        </table>
    </div>

    <h2>TV mode (experimental)</h2>
    <p>Turn it on with <code>&lt;BxApp SpatialNavigation="true" /&gt;</code> (or <code>Blux.setSpatialNavigation(true)</code>, or <code>data-bx-tv</code> on <code>html</code>). The arrow keys move focus to the nearest focusable element in that direction; widgets that use the arrows (open menus, tabs, listboxes) keep them, the topmost modal keeps focus inside; Enter activates; Back closes the topmost overlay. <code>html[data-bx-tv]</code> scales the UI by <code>--bx-tv-scale</code> (1.25), keeps content inside the overscan margin <code>--bx-tv-safe</code> (5%, as Android TV recommends: 48dp on the sides and 27dp at the top and bottom of a 960 x 540dp screen) and draws a thick focus ring.</p>
    <p>Fields never trap the arrows: a forwarded key has no default action, so a text field or a select that kept the arrows would keep the focus for good. Select/Enter puts a field in <em>editing mode</em> (<code>data-bx-editing</code>): a text field or textarea gets the caret (the arrows then move it; Enter is a line break in a textarea), a number, a range and a native select take the arrows as value changes. Back, or Enter in a single line field, leaves it, and the arrows move focus again. A <code>BxSelect</code> opens only with Select/Enter in TV mode, never with the arrows.</p>
    <p><strong>Soft keyboard.</strong> Focusing a field from a script does not reliably open the keyboard in a web view. <code>Blux.tvTextEditing()</code> tells whether a field that is typed into is in editing mode (the page also raises <code>bx-tv-edit</code> on <code>document</code> and calls <code>Blux.onEditField(editing, keyboard)</code> when you define it). The sample&#39;s <code>MainActivity</code> reads it after every Enter and after Back, and calls <code>WebView.RequestFocus()</code> then <code>InputMethodManager.ShowSoftInput(webView, ShowFlags.Implicit)</code> while a text field is edited, and <code>HideSoftInputFromWindow</code> when editing ends.</p>
    <p>A web view on Android TV may not receive the D-pad as key events, so the Activity forwards them into the page with <code>Blux.tvKey('ArrowLeft')</code>, and asks <code>Blux.tvBack()</code> on Back, leaving the app only when it returns false. The sample's <code>MainActivity</code> does this, and its manifest declares the leanback launcher with <code>touchscreen</code> not required, so one APK installs on phones and TVs. <code>-p:BluxTv=true</code> forces TV mode on any device.</p>

    <h2>Not available</h2>
    <ul>
        <li>Static rendering, prerendering, enhanced navigation, the culture cookie and <code>Blux.UI.Images.Local</code>.</li>
        <li>The inline head scripts of the web: the color mode is applied by the initializer instead.</li>
    </ul>
</div>

@code {
    private const string Program = """
        builder.Services.AddMauiBlazorWebView();
        builder.Services.AddBluxUi(o => o.Colors.Primary = BxPalette.Blue); // the same call as the web host
        builder.Services.AddSingleton<IBxCultureStore, PreferencesCultureStore>(); // optional
        """;

    private const string Index = """
        <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0, viewport-fit=cover" />
        <base href="/" />
        <link rel="stylesheet" href="_content/Blux.UI/blux.preflight.css" />
        <link rel="stylesheet" href="_content/Blux.UI/blux.css" />
        ...
        <div id="app">Loading...</div>
        <script src="_framework/blazor.webview.js" autostart="false"></script>
        """;

    private const string Xaml = """
        <BlazorWebView HostPage="wwwroot/index.html">
            <BlazorWebView.RootComponents>
                <RootComponent Selector="#app" ComponentType="{x:Type shared:Routes}" />
                <RootComponent Selector="head::after" ComponentType="{x:Type web:HeadOutlet}" />
            </BlazorWebView.RootComponents>
        </BlazorWebView>
        """;

    private const string Store = """
        public sealed class PreferencesCultureStore : IBxCultureStore
        {
            public ValueTask<string?> GetAsync() => ValueTask.FromResult(Preferences.Default.Get<string?>("blux-culture", null));
            public ValueTask SetAsync(string culture) { Preferences.Default.Set("blux-culture", culture); return ValueTask.CompletedTask; }
        }
        // MauiProgram, before the first page:
        // CultureInfo.DefaultThreadCurrentCulture = CultureInfo.DefaultThreadCurrentUICulture = new CultureInfo(Preferences.Default.Get("blux-culture", "en"));
        """;
}
